  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">India has ratified 47 ILO Conventions, including 6 of 8 Core and 3 Governance Conventions. However, critical instruments remain unratified, notably C29, C87, C98, C155, and C187. unilateral government decisions. Key labour standards under prolonged discussion include conventions on freedom of association, collective bargaining, occupational safety, domestic workers, and forced labour. Despite efforts, including a significant 2023 tripartite event marking the 75th anniversary of the UDHR and C87, progress towards ratification has been slow. Gap analyses for C155 (2018) and P29 (2019) need to be taken up.
While India has met its reporting obligations to ILO supervisory bodies, it can strengthen implementation of CEACR recommendations. The outdated ratifications, such as C45, need to be replaced by adopting modern OSH standards. The ILO Governing Body has urged India to prioritize ratification of up-to-date instruments like C155, P155, C161, C176, and C187, and to replace outdated conventions through remedial ratifications.
CPO Strategy 2024-2025
The CPO will drive targeted advocacy to revitalize tripartite dialogue and push for priority ratifications, focusing on P29, C87, C98, C155, and C187. Efforts will include organizing sensitization events, enhancing technical capacities, and updating gap analyses in collaboration with constituents.
Key actions include:
&#x2022;	Promoting ratification of core and technical conventions aligned with GB and SRM TWG recommendations.
&#x2022;	Supporting tripartite engagement in developing ILO standards, particularly for the platform economy.
&#x2022;	Providing technical assistance for the implementation of supervisory body recommendations and facilitating reporting, especially for Workers&#x2019; representatives.
&#x2022;	Implementing targeted interventions to protect vulnerable worker groups, ensuring a rights-based approach to labour governance.
Reactivating social dialogue mechanisms, addressing legal gaps, and ensuring compliance with international labour</narrative>
  </description>
